    "relatedServiceSlug": "south-india-road-journeys",
    "content": "<p>Fifteen days can create an unusually varied holiday. A 15-day South India road trip can take travellers from historic cities to hills, temples, backwaters, beaches and coastal towns.</p><p>One possible route could begin with Karnataka's heritage sites, continue towards Tamil Nadu's temple towns, cross the Western Ghats and end around Kerala or the coast.</p><p>But there is no reason to follow a fixed template.</p><p>An architecture enthusiast may spend several days around historic monuments. A family might prefer fewer destinations with longer stays. A couple looking for relaxation may prioritise beaches and scenic drives.</p><p>How do you plan a 15-day road trip itinerary?</p><p>The secret is geographical logic.</p><p>Instead of jumping between distant destinations, build a route where each stop naturally leads to the next. Choose hotels that reduce unnecessary backtracking and avoid changing accommodation simply for the sake of adding another name to the itinerary.</p><p>A multi-state road trip in India can be particularly rewarding when different landscapes are connected logically rather than treated as separate holidays.</p><p>Travel time can also become part of the experience. A chauffeur-driven journey allows passengers to rest, photograph landscapes, read or simply watch the scenery.</p><p>Suhalaya Travels can support customised chauffeur-driven journeys and assist with route and stay planning where required.</p><p>Fifteen days is long enough to create variety, but short enough to require choices.</p><p>Pick the experiences that matter most.</p><p>Then let the itinerary connect them.</p>"
